<DocsLayout
  title="Inference providers"
  eyebrow="Architecture"
  description="Provider extensions translate model APIs into canonical Roder inference events."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/sessions/", title: "Sessions & state" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/memories/", title: "Memory & embeddings" }}
>
  <p>
    Providers are native extensions that implement <code>InferenceEngine</code>. The runtime
    selects an engine by provider id, sends it a canonical Roder turn, and receives
    canonical inference events back. Long sessions respect provider compaction boundaries
    so compacted state stays compact instead of replaying the full pre-compact history on
    later turns.
  </p>

  <h2>Default provider set</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>roder-ext-openai-responses</code> - OpenAI Responses-style inference, including GPT-6.1 catalog entries and Ultra effort mapping.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-openai-chat-completions</code> - chat-completions-compatible endpoints.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-anthropic</code> - Anthropic Messages-style inference.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-claude-code</code> - the local Claude Code CLI, with resumable sessions and optional Claude-in-Chrome browser tools.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-gemini</code> - Gemini through the configured Google-compatible API-key path, including Gemini 3.8 Flash and Gemini 3.7 Flash.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-vertex</code> - Gemini-on-Vertex through service-account OAuth and regional Vertex AI endpoints, including the same Flash catalog.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-xai</code> - SuperGrok OAuth and direct xAI API-key inference, with Grok 4.6 as the default model.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-cursor</code> - Cursor Composer and proxied frontier models, including Grok 4.6, Gemini 3.7 Flash, and Claude Fable 5.1, through Cursor AgentService.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-kimi-code</code> - Kimi Code through subscription OAuth or API-key auth.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-openrouter</code> - OpenRouter API-key provider, defaulting to the <code>x-ai/grok-4.6</code> route.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-roder-cloud</code> - roder.cloud hosted inference using a roder_ team key exchanged for short-lived JWTs.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-opencode</code> - OpenCode Zen and Go subscription providers, including current DeepSeek paid routes and free Zen catalog entries.</li>
    <li><code>roder-ext-deepseek</code> - DeepSeek Platform through <code>DEEPSEEK_API_KEY</code> and the built-in DeepSeek model catalog.</li>
    <li><code>roder-core::fake_provider</code> - deterministic mock behavior for tests and offline work.</li>
    <li><code>roder-codex-auth</code> and host wiring - Codex OAuth with GPT-6.1 Sol and Terra, plus other advertised models.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Claude Code and Claude in Chrome</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/claude-code/"><code>claude-code</code> provider</a> runs the local
    Claude Code CLI under Roder while reusing its session between turns. If Claude's Chrome
    extension is paired, Roder can automatically enable the CLI's native browser tools for
    {" "}<code>claude-code/*</code> models. Those calls run through Claude Code and appear as hosted
    tool activity in Roder, while every other unmapped CLI tool remains denied.
  </p>

  <h2>Fireworks provider</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/fireworks/">Fireworks AI</a> is a first-party provider with canonical
    id <code>fireworks</code>. It uses Fireworks-specific credentials, preserves
    slash-bearing <code>accounts/fireworks/models/...</code> ids, and streams through the
    OpenAI-compatible Responses adapter. Offline metadata keeps it visible in provider
    menus before a key is configured.
  </p>

  <h2>OpenAI service tiers</h2>
  <p>
    Set the OpenAI API provider default in user configuration. Explicit request tiers
    take precedence, and generated remote app servers receive this setting too.
    Provider and model support determine whether a requested tier is available.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`[providers.openai]
service_tier = "ultrafast"`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Clients can also persist a tier for a thread or override one turn through the
    <a href="/docs/app-server/#inference-service-tiers">app-server service-tier methods</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Codex OAuth</h2>
  <p>
    The Codex provider catalog includes <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> and
    <code>gpt-6.1-terra</code>. New Codex-backed sessions default to
    {" "}<code>gpt-6.1-sol</code>. Terra has a 1M context window.
    The retired <code>gpt-6-sol</code> remains resolvable but is hidden from selectors.
    Use the running provider's model catalog for availability.
    Supported Astra, Sol, and Terra models support Roder's <code>ultra</code>
    effort, which remains visible in Roder while the Responses transport maps it to
    the provider's <code>max</code> reasoning effort. Ultra also turns on bounded
    proactive delegation for those models. Spawned teammates inherit the live Codex
    model, Ultra effort, policy, runner, tools, and workspace unless the spawn requests
    a fresh-context compatible override; lower efforts keep teammate spawning
    explicit-request-only.
  </p>

  <p>
    Codex and other OpenAI Responses-backed providers preserve opaque provider-side
    compaction items in the transcript. Once a compaction item appears, Roder treats it as
    the next turn's context boundary: the compact state and new work are sent forward, while
    older conversation and tool output stay in the durable session record instead of being
    re-sent to the model.
  </p>

  <pre><code>roder auth login codex
RODER_PROVIDER=codex RODER_MODEL=gpt-6-astra cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_PROVIDER=codex RODER_MODEL=gpt-6.1-terra RODER_REASONING=ultra cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2 id="cursor-agentservice">Cursor AgentService</h2>
  <p>
    Cursor is now a native provider id. Roder exchanges a Cursor User API Key, calls the
    HTTP/2 AgentService path directly, streams text and thinking metadata, and maps
    Cursor-native read, edit, shell, grep, and glob requests into canonical Roder tools.
    Agentic Cursor turns still execute through Roder policy and tool routing rather than
    bypassing the runtime.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catalog includes <code>composer-2.5-fast</code>, <code>gpt-5.5-fast</code>,
    {" "}<code>grok-4.6</code>, <code>gemini-3.7-flash</code>, and
    {" "}<code>claude-fable-5-1</code>. Fable 5.1 is exposed as a 1M-context
    Cursor-routed model with high reasoning by default and the full Opus-style effort
    range up to <code>xhigh</code> and <code>max</code>. Reasoning selections map to
    Cursor's <code>effort</code>, <code>thinking</code>, and <code>fast</code> request
    parameters, while a stable per-thread conversation id keeps multi-turn context
    attached to the right Roder thread.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cursor discovery and caches filter retired model ids. The catalog also includes
    Gemini 3.1 Pro and Gemini 4 Argon with supported reasoning settings. Native and MCP
    tool calls use policy-approved runtime results and retain thread context across turns.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export CURSOR_API_KEY="..."
RODER_PROVIDER=cursor RODER_MODEL=cursor/composer-2.5 cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>Kimi Code auth</h2>
  <p>
    Kimi Code can use the managed subscription OAuth flow or a saved API key. The CLI
    supports both through <code>roder auth login kimi-code</code>; pass
    {" "}<code>--api-key</code> to paste or provide a key directly, or <code>--oauth</code> to force
    the browser-based subscription flow. Status reports API-key configuration, and logout
    clears the saved provider key.
  </p>

  <pre><code>roder auth login kimi-code --api-key
roder auth status kimi-code
roder auth logout kimi-code</code></pre>

  <h2>xAI and SuperGrok</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>roder-ext-xai</code> provider installs both the direct <code>xai</code>
    API-key surface and the OAuth-backed <code>supergrok</code> surface. Both catalogs
    default to <code>grok-4.7</code>, exposed with a 500k context window, high reasoning
    by default, and explicit <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>,
    and <code>xhigh</code> reasoning choices. Roder treats Grok maximum-prompt-length
    failures as context-limit failures, compacts, and retries the live turn before asking
    the user to intervene. Older Grok 4.3, Grok 4.20, and SuperGrok Composer entries
    remain selectable for pinned workflows.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export XAI_API_KEY="..."
RODER_PROVIDER=xai RODER_MODEL=grok-4.6 RODER_REASONING=xhigh cargo run -p roder-cli

roder auth login supergrok
RODER_PROVIDER=supergrok RODER_MODEL=grok-4.6 cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>OpenRouter and Grok</h2>
  <p>
    OpenRouter is a first-class provider id. The built-in catalog includes
    {" "}<code>x-ai/grok-4.6</code>, a 500k-context Grok route that supports tools,
    structured outputs, image input, compaction, and patch-style edits. Roder keeps the
    OpenRouter model slug intact, so clients should keep provider and model fields separate
    instead of splitting every model id on <code>/</code>. Its reasoning options are
    {" "}<code>none</code>, <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, and <code>high</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>roder.cloud and Vertex</h2>
  <p>
    Roder now includes two infrastructure-oriented provider paths. The
    {" "}<a href="/docs/roder-cloud/">roder.cloud provider</a> routes through Roder's hosted
    inference service, using a synchronous Responses-style subset and a short-lived
    token exchange. The <a href="/docs/vertex/">Vertex provider</a> runs Gemini models
    through Google Cloud regional endpoints with service-account OAuth, so enterprises
    can use Vertex access without switching to the Gemini API-key provider.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export OPENROUTER_API_KEY="..."
RODER_PROVIDER=openrouter RODER_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.6 cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/openrouter/">OpenRouter setup</a> for environment variables,
    optional attribution headers, model discovery, and app-server configuration behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>OpenCode Zen and Go</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>roder-ext-opencode</code> provider exposes OpenCode as two provider ids:
    {" "}<code>opencode</code> for Zen and <code>opencode-go</code> for Go. Both use OpenCode
    API keys and a chat-completions-style streaming API. The built-in Zen catalog now
    omits disabled free DeepSeek ids, includes current free models such as
    {" "}<code>big-pickle</code>, <code>mimo-v2.5-free</code>,
    {" "}<code>nemotron-3-ultra-free</code>, and <code>north-mini-code-free</code>, and exposes
    paid <code>deepseek-v4-flash</code> / <code>deepseek-v4-pro</code> routes where the
    account supports them.
  </p>

  <p>
    OpenCode-backed DeepSeek turns can run longer multi-step tool workflows because Roder
    coalesces parallel tool calls into a valid assistant tool-call message and pairs every
    id with a tool result before the next model step. When OpenCode rejects a request,
    Roder surfaces structured provider details such as disabled-model and billing errors
    so users can fix catalog selection or account setup directly.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export OPENCODE_API_KEY="..."
RODER_PROVIDER=opencode RODER_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_PROVIDER=opencode-go RODER_MODEL=deepseek-v4-pro cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>DeepSeek Platform</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>roder-ext-deepseek</code> provider exposes DeepSeek Platform directly as
    provider id <code>deepseek</code>. It uses DeepSeek's OpenAI-compatible Chat
    Completions API at <code>https://api.deepseek.com/v1</code>, reads
    {" "}<code>DEEPSEEK_API_KEY</code> or <code>RODER_DEEPSEEK_API_KEY</code>, and appears in
    the normal provider/model catalog without custom endpoint configuration.
  </p>

  <p>
    The built-in models are <code>deepseek-chat</code>, <code>deepseek-reasoner</code>,
    {" "}<code>deepseek-v4-flash</code>, and <code>deepseek-v4-pro</code>. The chat and flash
    entries use non-thinking defaults for straightforward coding turns. The reasoner and
    pro entries expose Roder reasoning options for tasks that benefit from longer
    deliberation.
  </p>

  <p>
    For thinking-mode turns, Roder sends DeepSeek's explicit <code>thinking</code> toggle,
    maps the selected Roder reasoning effort to the provider request, streams
    {" "}<code>reasoning_content</code> into the reasoning timeline, and preserves that
    reasoning content across tool-call turns when DeepSeek requires it.
  </p>

  <pre><code>export DEEPSEEK_API_KEY="..."
RODER_PROVIDER=deepseek RODER_MODEL=deepseek-chat cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_PROVIDER=deepseek RODER_MODEL=deepseek-reasoner RODER_REASONING=medium cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>Selecting defaults</h2>
  <pre><code># ~/.roder/config.toml
provider = "codex"
model = "gpt-6.1-sol"
reasoning = "medium"</code></pre>

  <pre><code>RODER_PROVIDER=anthropic RODER_MODEL=claude-opus-4-5 cargo run -p roder-cli
RODER_PROVIDER=mock cargo run -p roder-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>Embedding providers</h2>
  <p>
    The memory subsystem uses provider-neutral embedding backends. The first-party
    remote providers include OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3-large</code>, Google
    <code>gemini-embedding-2</code>, and ZeroEntropy <code>zembed-1</code>. The shared
    embedding contract carries query/document intent so retrieval providers can tune
    memory lookups without leaking provider-specific request shapes into the runtime.
  </p>

  <pre><code>roder memory providers set google --model gemini-embedding-2
roder memory providers set zeroentropy --model zembed-1</code></pre>

  <h2>Provider contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Advertise metadata, auth type, capabilities, and model descriptors.</li>
    <li>Stream text, tool calls, errors, and completion as canonical <code>InferenceEvent</code> values.</li>
    <li>Mark local or provider-side compaction records as hard context boundaries for later turns.</li>
    <li>Preserve tool-call ids across the turn so the runtime can route results back correctly.</li>
    <li>Handle cancellation promptly when the active turn is interrupted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Compaction behavior</h2>
  <p>
    Roder recognizes both local <code>ContextCompaction</code> summaries and OpenAI
    Responses provider metadata items whose output contains a compaction record. The runtime
    trims later provider requests to the latest boundary, preventing repeated server-side
    compaction loops in very long sessions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Anthropic adapter also maps local context summaries into Messages API input on the
    emergency client-side path. That keeps the summary visible to Claude models while
    dropping older pre-summary chat, tool calls, and tool output from the active request
    window.
  </p>

  <h2>Media providers</h2>
  <p>
    Image generation providers are deliberately separate from chat inference providers.
    OpenAI GPT Image 2.5 Flare (the OpenAI default), Sunburst and Google Gemini image models live in the media catalog and are
    selected through <a href="/docs/media-generation/">media generation</a>, not the chat
    model picker.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Provider-specific APIs stay outside the core.</strong> Responses, Messages,
    Gemini, Claude Code, Cursor AgentService, OpenRouter, Fireworks, and chat-completions shapes are adapter details, not runtime types.
  </div>
  <h2>Custom Responses endpoint capabilities</h2>
  <p>
    Custom Responses-compatible providers can declare native V2 compaction and a
    hosted-web access ceiling. These settings constrain requests; they do not grant
    extra tools or permissions.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`[providers.my_endpoint]
base_url = "https://responses.example/v1"
api_key_env = "MY_ENDPOINT_API_KEY"

[providers.my_endpoint.capabilities]
remote_compaction = "v2"
external_web_access = false`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>remote_compaction</code> accepts <code>unsupported</code> or <code>v2</code>.
    Omitted settings keep custom endpoints on local compaction; OpenAI and Codex
    require native compaction. V2 endpoints must return a valid opaque compaction
    item and successful terminal completion before Roder commits the new boundary.
    Failed or interrupted compaction retains original history, including across
    restart; reported usage still counts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>external_web_access = false</code> caps live hosted search at cached
    results, including during native compaction. It never enables disabled search.
    Omission or <code>true</code> preserves the caller's requested mode.
    See the <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/master/docs/custom-responses-providers.md">endpoint capability contract</a>
    for protocol and fallback requirements.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
